        '3-5', $q$Masters of Disguise$q$, $q$(1) Octopuses are sea animals with eight arms and no bones. (2) They live in oceans all over the world, and many of them are experts at hiding. (3) My cousin saw an octopus at an aquarium last summer.

(4) An octopus's skin is packed with thousands of chromatophores. (5) A chromatophore is a tiny sac filled with color that can stretch open or squeeze shut. (6) When the sacs open, the octopus can turn red, brown, or yellow in less than a second.

(7) Color is only part of the trick. (8) An octopus can change the texture of its skin. (9) It can raise small bumps so that it looks like a rough rock or a clump of seaweed. (10) A marine scientist named Dr. Lena Varga explained, "If you blink, you might lose track of it completely."

(11) These disguises help octopuses hide from hungry sharks and eels. (12) They also help octopuses sneak up on crabs and shrimp.$q$, 'draft')
