        '3-5', $q$Paper from a River Plant$q$, $q$(1) Egypt is a country in northern Africa. (2) Long ago, Egyptians wrote on a material called papyrus. (3) Papyrus was made from a tall plant that grew along the Nile River.

(4) Making papyrus took several steps. (5) Workers cut the green stalks and peeled off the tough outer skin. (6) They sliced the soft inside into long, thin strips. (7) Next, the strips were laid side by side, and a second layer was placed across them. (8) Workers pressed the layers flat and let them dry in the sun. (9) The plant's own sticky juice held the sheet together.

(10) Papyrus was strong and light. (11) Sheets could be glued end to end to make long scrolls. (12) Some scrolls were longer than a school bus. (13) Scribes used papyrus to write down lots of things. (14) Egyptians also traded papyrus to other lands.$q$, 'draft')
